From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ai_re <ai_re@localhost>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 04:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] arm: add a chainloaded Annapurna Labs Alpine V2 target

A second-stage U-Boot for Alpine V2 boards (4x Cortex-A57), started by
the vendor's U-Boot 2015.07 (al-boot 2.60) after al-boot has trained
DRAM, set up SerDes and the PCIe unit adapters and installed its PSCI
v0.1 monitor. No low-level init:

- position independent, with a Linux Image header, so it runs wherever
  it is loaded on a 4 KiB boundary (also booti-able by itself);
- save_boot_params: entered by "go" with the vendor MMU and caches on,
  clean the D-cache by set/way and turn them off before start.S
  continues; "bootm" of a kernel uImage arrives with them off already.
  The entry EL, SCTLR and x0 are printed by checkboard();
- DRAM banks from /memory; mem_map = DRAM as Normal, every hole below
  4 GiB as Device (built after relocation from gd->dram);
- /reserved-memory ranges of the control DT keep ram_top (and so the
  relocated U-Boot) clear, and ft_board_setup() copies them into the OS
  DTB's memory reserve map, since a kernel-tree DTB knows nothing
  about the firmware;
- no CFG_SYS_SDRAM_BASE, so the /memory base is not overridden;
- load addresses for bootstd/extlinux/booti in the board env, fdtfile
  from CONFIG_DEFAULT_FDT_FILE;
- alpine-v2-u-boot.dtsi: bootph-all for the console path of the
  mainline alpine-v2.dtsi, for a board's -u-boot.dtsi to include.

booti/bootm otherwise copy the initrd and the FDT to the top of the
first bank. The load addresses in the env already keep them clear of
the kernel and of reserved memory, so skip the copy (saves copying a
large initrd, and nothing lands near the firmware ranges). The
"don't relocate" value must be the 64-bit all-ones: 0xffffffff is
read as a 4 GiB limit on arm64.

Scope: alpine-v2.
---
